%[text] %[text:anchor:T_6A95D682] # Mesh Analysis Lab
%[text] [⇦ DC Circuit Analysis Main Menu](file:../MainMenu.m)
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"**Table of Contents**"}
%[text] In this lab, you will perform a mesh analysis of the linear circuit shown below in MATLAB. Afterward, you will build the circuit in Simscape and take current measurements to verify your result. Before completing this assignment, consider reviewing [MeshAnalysis.m](<matlab:open(fullfile(currentProject().RootFolder,'Scripts','MeshAnalysis.m'))>).
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![circuit2.png](text:image:5959)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Circuit to analyze*
%[text] The resistances and voltages are
%[text]  $\\begin{array}{l}\nR\_1 = 100 \\ \\Omega\n\\\\\nR\_2 = 10 \\ \\Omega\n\\\\\nR\_3 = 220 \\ \\Omega\n\\\\\nR\_4 = 1 \\ \\Omega\n\\\\\nR\_5 = 200 \\ \\Omega\n\\\\\nR\_6 = 1 \\ \\text{k} \\Omega\n\\end{array}${"altText":"\begin{array}{l} R\_1 = 100 \ \Omega \\ R\_2 = 10 \ \Omega \\ R\_3 = 220 \ \Omega \\ R\_4 = 1 \ \Omega \\ R\_5 = 200 \ \Omega \\ R\_6 = 1 \ \text{k} \Omega \end{array}"} $\\begin{array}{l}\nV\_1 = 12 \\ \\text{V}\n\\\\\nV\_2 = 3 \\ \\text{V}\n\\end{array}${"altText":"\begin{array}{l} V\_1 = 12 \ \text{V} \\ V\_2 = 3 \ \text{V} \end{array}"}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_20A0308B] ### **Part 1. Mesh analysis in MATLAB**
%[text] **Task 1.** On paper, draw the circuit diagram and add three mesh currents. Use the conventions shown below for the directions of the currents.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![circuit2\_loops.png](text:image:2763)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Current directions*
%[text] **Task 2.** Complete the mesh analysis by deriving the equation for each loop in terms of the resistances $R\_i${"altText":"R\_i"} and voltages $V\_i${"altText":"V\_i"}.
%[text] **Task 3.** Using the equations you derived in step 2, create the matrix form of the equations. Let the vector of unknown currents be
%[text]  $\\mathbf{I} = \\left\[ \\begin{array}{l} I\_1 \\\\ I\_2 \\\\ I\_3 \\end{array} \\right\]${"altText":"\mathbf{I} = \left\[ \begin{array}{l} I\_1 \\ I\_2 \\ I\_3 \end{array} \right\]"}
%[text] Then, define the matrix system as $\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{I} = \\mathbf{b}${"altText":"\mathbf{A} \mathbf{I} = \mathbf{b}"}. 
%[text] **Task 4.** In the space provided below, define the values of the resistors in ohms and the voltage sources in volts.
% Define R1, R2, R3, R4, R5, R6, V1, and V2

%[text] **Task 5.** With the resistances and voltages defined, you can define the matrix system in MATLAB. Define the coefficient matrix `A` and constant vector `b` in the space below in terms of `R1`, `R2`, `R3`, `R4`, `R5`, `R6`, `V1`, and `V2`. Then, solve for the vector of currents `I`.
% Write your code here

%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7CA594C4] ### **Part 2. Simscape model analysis**
%[text] In this section, you will check your result from **Part 1** by creating a model of the electrical circuit in Simscape and then measuring the currents.
%[text] **Task 6.** Open the model:
%[text]{"align":"center"} [![lab\_model.png](text:image:3b84)](<matlab: MeshAnalysisCircuit>)
%[text]{"align":"center"} [`MeshAnalysisCircuit.slx`](<matlab: MeshAnalysisCircuit>)
%[text] **Task 7.** Complete the model by connecting the circuit elements to match the diagram below.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![circuit2\_signs.png](text:image:7076)
%[text] **Task 8.** Use the multimeter to measure each of the mesh currents. For detailed instructions on using the virtual multimeter to measure currents, see the instructions in the last part of [MeshAnalysis.m](<matlab:open(fullfile(currentProject().RootFolder,'Scripts','MeshAnalysis.m'))>).
%[text] **Note:** Watch [this 2-minute instructional video](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/81383420/145589989-f7ae4a32-cab3-405d-b7e8-476e34d697b5.mp4) to learn how to use the virtual multimeter.
